    <custom_item>
      type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description     : "1.34 UBTU-24-200000"
      info            : "The operating system must limit the number of concurrent sessions to ten for all accounts and/or account types.

GROUP ID: V-270677
RULE ID: SV-270677r1066520

The operating system management includes the ability to control the number of users and user sessions that utilize an operating system. Limiting the number of allowed users and sessions per user is helpful in reducing the risks related to denial-of-service (DoS) attacks.

This requirement addresses concurrent sessions for information system accounts and does not address concurrent sessions by single users via multiple system accounts. The maximum number of concurrent sessions should be defined based upon mission needs and the operational environment for each system."
      solution        : "Configure the operating system to limit the number of concurrent sessions to 10 for all accounts and/or account types.

Add or modify the following line at the top of the \"/etc/security/limits.conf\" file or in a \".conf\" file defined in /etc/security/limits.d/:

* hard maxlogins 10"
      reference       : "800-53|AC-10,800-53r5|AC-10,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000054,CSCv7|5.1,CSCv8|4.1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|AC-10,LEVEL|3A,NESA|T5.5.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,Rule-ID|SV-270677r1066520_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-200000,Vuln-ID|V-270677"
      see_also        : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      file            : "/etc/security/limits.conf /etc/security/limits.d/*.conf"
      regex           : "^[\\s]*\\*[\\s]+hard[\\s]+maxlogins[\\s]+"
      expect          : "^[\\s]*\\*[\\s]+hard[\\s]+maxlogins[\\s]+([1-9]|10)[\\s]*$"
      required        : NO
      min_occurrences : "1"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so module is present in /etc/pam.d/common-auth file"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/common-auth"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*auth([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_faillock.so[\\s]+"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*auth([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_faillock.so[\\s]+"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so is used with audit"
          file        : "/etc/security/faillock.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*audit[\\s]*$"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*audit[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so is used with silent"
          file        : "/etc/security/faillock.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*silent[\\s]*$"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*silent[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so is used with deny set to 3 or less"
          file        : "/etc/security/faillock.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*deny[\\s]*="
          expect      : "^[\\s]*deny[\\s]*=[\\s]*@PASSWORD_LOCKOUT_DENY@[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so is used with fail_interval set to 900 or less"
          file        : "/etc/security/faillock.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*fail_interval[\\s]*="
          expect      : "^[\\s]*fail_interval[\\s]*=[\\s]*@PAM_FAIL_INTERVAL@[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so is used with unlock_time set to 0"
          file        : "/etc/security/faillock.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*unlock_time[\\s]*="
          expect      : "^[\\s]*unlock_time[\\s]*=[\\s]*@PAM_LOCKOUT_VALUE@[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.47 UBTU-24-200610"
          info        : "The operating system must automatically lock an account until the locked account is released by an administrator when three unsuccessful logon attempts have been made.

GROUP ID: V-270690
RULE ID: SV-270690r1067126

By limiting the number of failed logon attempts, the risk of unauthorized system access via user password guessing, otherwise known as brute-forcing, is reduced. Limits are imposed by locking the account.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000021-GPOS-00005, SRG-OS-000329-GPOS-00128"
          solution    : "Configure the operating system to utilize the \"pam_faillock\" module.

Add or modify the following lines in the \"/etc/pam.d/common-auth\" file, below the \"auth\" definition for \"pam_unix.so\":

auth [default=die] pam_faillock.so authfail
auth sufficient pam_faillock.so authsucc

Configure the \"pam_faillock\" module to use the following options.

Add or modify the following lines in the \"/etc/security/faillock.conf\" file:

audit
silent
deny = 3
fail_interval = 900
unlock_time = 0"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.8,800-171r3|03.01.08a.,800-171r3|03.01.08b.,800-53|AC-7a.,800-53|AC-7b.,800-53r5|AC-7a.,800-53r5|AC-7b.,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000044,CCI|CCI-002238,CN-L3|7.1.2.7(f),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(b),CSCv7|16.7,CSCv8|6.2,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.5,ITSG-33|AC-7a.,ITSG-33|AC-7b.,LEVEL|3A,NESA|T5.5.1,NIAv2|AM24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|8.1.6,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|8.1.7,PCI-DSSv4.0|8.3.4,Rule-ID|SV-270690r1067126_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-200610,TBA-FIISB|36.2.4,TBA-FIISB|45.1.2,TBA-FIISB|45.2.1,TBA-FIISB|45.2.2,Vuln-ID|V-270690"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
      description : "1.52 UBTU-24-300001"
      info        : "The operating system must be configured so that the Advance Package Tool (APT) prevents the installation of patches, service packs, device drivers, or operating system components without verification they have been digitally signed using a certificate that is recognized and approved by the organization.

GROUP ID: V-270695
RULE ID: SV-270695r1066574

Changes to any software components can have significant effects on the overall security of the operating system. This requirement ensures the software has not been tampered with and that it has been provided by a trusted vendor.

Accordingly, patches, service packs, device drivers, or operating system components must be signed with a certificate recognized and approved by the organization.

Verifying the authenticity of the software prior to installation validates the integrity of the patch or upgrade received from a vendor. This ensures the software has not been tampered with and that it has been provided by a trusted vendor. Self-signed certificates are disallowed by this requirement. The operating system should not have to verify the software again. This requirement does not mandate DOD certificates for this purpose; however, the certificate used to verify the software must be from an approved certificate authority (CA)."
      solution    : "Configure APT to prevent the installation of patches, service packs, device drivers, or Ubuntu 24.04 LTS components without verification they have been digitally signed using a certificate recognized and approved by the organization.

Remove/update any APT configuration files that contain the variable \"AllowUnauthenticated\" to \"false\" or remove \"AllowUnauthenticated\" entirely from each file. Below is an example of setting the \"AllowUnauthenticated\" variable to \"false\":

APT::Get::AllowUnauthenticated \"false\";"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.5,800-171r3|03.04.05,800-53|CM-5(3),800-53r5|CM-14,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-001749,CCI|CCI-003992,CSCv7|3.4,CSCv8|7.3,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.19,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|CM-5(3),LEVEL|3A,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.5.3,NESA|T7.6.3,QCSC-v1|7.2,Rule-ID|SV-270695r1066574_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-300001,Vuln-ID|V-270695"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      file        : "/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/*"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*APT::Get::AllowUnauthenticated[\\s]+"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*APT::Get::AllowUnauthenticated[\\s]+[\"']*true[\"']*[\\s]*;"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.63 UBTU-24-300017"
      info        : "The operating system must enforce a delay of at least four seconds between logon prompts following a failed logon attempt.

GROUP ID: V-270706
RULE ID: SV-270706r1068361

Limiting the number of logon attempts over a certain time interval reduces the chances that an unauthorized user may gain access to an account."
      solution    : "Configure the operating system to enforce a delay of at least four seconds between logon prompts following a failed logon attempt.

Add or modify the following line in the \"/etc/pam.d/common-auth\" file:

auth required pam_faildelay.so delay=4000000"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSCv7|5.1,CSCv8|4.1,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|3A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-270706r1068361_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-300017,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-270706"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      file        : "/etc/pam.d/common-auth"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*auth[\\s]*required[\\s]*pam_faildelay\\.so"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*auth[\\s]*required[\\s]*pam_faildelay\\.so+([^#\\n\\r]+)delay=@PAM_FAILDELAY@"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_lastlog.so required"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/login"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*session[\\s]+required[\\s]+pam_lastlog\\.so"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*session[\\s]+required[\\s]+pam_lastlog\\.so"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
          description : "pam_lastlog.so silent option"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/login"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*session([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_lastlog\\.so([^#\\n\\r]+)silent"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*session([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_lastlog\\.so([^#\\n\\r]+)silent"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.67 UBTU-24-300024"
          info        : "Ubuntu 24.04 LTS must display the date and time of the last successful account logon upon logon.

GROUP ID: V-270710
RULE ID: SV-270710r1066619

Configuration settings are the set of parameters that can be changed in hardware, software, or firmware components of the system that affect the security posture and/or functionality of the system. Security-related parameters are those parameters impacting the security state of the system, including the parameters required to satisfy other security control requirements. Security-related parameters include, for example, registry settings; account, file, directory permission settings; and settings for functions, ports, protocols, services, and remote connections."
          solution    : "Configure Ubuntu 24.04 LTS to provide users with feedback on when account accesses last occurred by setting the required configuration options in \"/etc/pam.d/login\".

Add the following line to the top of \"/etc/pam.d/login\":

session required pam_lastlog.so showfailed"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|III,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|3A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-270710r1066619_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-300024,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-270710"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description     : "1.91 UBTU-24-400340"
      info            : "The operating system must be configured such that Pluggable Authentication Module (PAM) prohibits the use of cached authentications after one day.

GROUP ID: V-270734
RULE ID: SV-270734r1066691

If cached authentication information is out-of-date, the validity of the authentication information may be questionable."
      solution        : "Configure PAM to prohibit the use of cached authentications after one day.

Add or modify the following line in the \"/etc/sssd/sssd.conf\" file, just below the line \"[pam]\":

offline_credentials_expiration = 1

Note: It is valid for this configuration to be in a file with a name that ends with \".conf\" and does not begin with a \".\" in the \"/etc/sssd/conf.d/\" directory instead of the \"/etc/sssd/sssd.conf\" file."
      reference       : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.12,800-53|IA-5(13),800-53r5|IA-5(13),CAT|III,CCI|CCI-002007,CSCv7|5.1,CSCv8|4.1,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5,LEVEL|3A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-270734r1066691_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-400340,Vuln-ID|V-270734"
      see_also        : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      file            : "/etc/sssd/sssd.conf /etc/sssd/conf.d/*.conf"
      regex           : "^[\\s]*offline_credentials_expiration[\\s]*="
      expect          : "^[\\s]*offline_credentials_expiration[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
      required        : NO
      min_occurrences : "1"
    </custom_item>
  </then>

  <else>
    <report type:"WARNING">
      description : "CIS_Ubuntu_Linux_24.04_LTS_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_III.audit from CIS Ubuntu Linux 24.04 LTS STIG v1.0.0"
